From: RitzDaCat <49771174+RitzDaCat@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] utils/precisetimer: arm non-positive deadlines

timerfd_settime() disarms the timer when it_value is zero and fails with
EINVAL for a negative value, which PreciseTimer::start() turns into
qFatal(). Clamp a deadline at or before the monotonic epoch to 1 ns so the
timer arms and expires at once, as a past deadline should.

Split out of 0019 for KWin 6.8; it does not depend on the rest of the
series.
---
 src/utils/precisetimer.cpp | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/utils/precisetimer.cpp b/src/utils/precisetimer.cpp
index 7e274547a221aac04544004bcfd3420275b8c371..14db9b46d72ea0eab2f4269741f71e4ee844790e 100644
--- a/src/utils/precisetimer.cpp
+++ b/src/utils/precisetimer.cpp
@@ -43,6 +43,13 @@ void PreciseTimer::start(std::chrono::nanoseconds deadline)
 {
     drain();
 
+    // timerfd disarms on it_value == {0,0} and rejects negative values, so a
+    // deadline at or before the monotonic epoch must clamp to 1ns to arm and
+    // expire immediately instead of never firing (or aborting in qFatal).
+    if (deadline <= std::chrono::nanoseconds::zero()) {
+        deadline = std::chrono::nanoseconds(1);
+    }
+
     const auto seconds = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::seconds>(deadline);
     const auto nanoseconds = deadline - seconds;
     itimerspec spec = {};
